\section{Introduction}
As the project aims to investigate the effects of gestures on touch-based gaming, a recognition unit to recognise these gestures was needed. Two gesture engines were developed for this purpose. One of these used a Hidden Markov Model, while the other used an approach based on state machines. Both these engines, however, share a common feature extraction unit. The following sections will describe and discuss what feature extraction is as well as the specific design limitations placed on an implementation for the iPad. As a number of different techniques were tested, the implementation of each as well as a comparison of their robustness and consistency is also included.


\section{Background}
The fundemental idea behind feature-based image processing techniques is that of data or dimensionality reduction. That is, complex data is reduced to a simpler and more compact representation~\citep{Guyo06}. As the nature of both the representation and the process of reduction is highly dependant on the problem at hand, this section will provide only a high level introduction to these concepts before discussing techniques specifically relevant to feature extraction for touch-based gesture recognition.

\subsection{Features and their detection, extraction and segmentation}
There is no universal definition of what consititutes a feature. Rather, they can be thought of as a fundemental unit with a higher-level interpretation relevant in the context of computationally solving a specific problem~\citep{Erda08}. Thus a feature could be a single point, line, texture, shape or other appropriate sub-image description~\citep{Mara10}. The type of feature required also determines the method of feature extraction needed. 

Feature extraction is a very broad topic primarily concerned with locating and describing features within the domain of image processing~\citep{Erda08}. Feature detection and segmentation are two other related and often overlapping topics in this field. Feature detection refers to locating areas or elements of interest within an image~\citep{Guyo06}. This is of importance in images that contain much data. Segmentation pertains to the process of partitioning information into a number of segments or features~\citep{Nixo08}. In general, both detection and segmentation are used when extracting features. Finally, features need to be related to each other in some way in order to fully describe the subimage they represent~\citep{Mara10}. The way in which this is done is also highly dependant on the nature of the features and the underlying object they represent. Thus, when considering these techniques, it is vital that the specific charectoristics and requirements of the data in question be fully understood.

\subsection{Curve fitting}
Curve fitting refers to constructing a curve or function that best describes a set of points~\citep{Akim70}. Thus it can be seen as a form of dimensionality reduction. There are a number of ways of achieving this effect, however, as this is a topic in its own right, only an overview of the basic premises will be provided. Figure~\ref{curves} provides a graphical overview of some of the techniques discussed below.

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \subfloat[Interpolation]{\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{interpolation.pdf}}       \hspace{20pt}   \vspace{10pt}       
  \subfloat[Smoothing]{\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{smoothing.pdf}} \hspace{20pt}
  \subfloat[Splines]{\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{spline.pdf}} \hspace{20pt}   
  \subfloat[Straight-line regression]{\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{lineRegression.pdf}} \hspace{20pt}     
  \subfloat[Parabolic regression]{\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{parabolicRegression.pdf}} \hspace{20pt}
  
  \caption{Illustration of various curve fitting techniques. The black dots represent the data to be fitted, with the gray line showing the fitted curve.}
  \label{curves}
\end{figure}


One of these concepts is interpolation. Interpolation refers to using existing data points to determine a function, or similar, which passes through all the given data points as well as defining the values of those not present~\citep{Mole04}. Thus it can be thought of as a means of defining a function that takes on specific values at certain points. Therefore interpolation is different to other curve fitting methods as it requires that the function pass exactly through all the data points provided~\citep{Akim70}. Another curve fitting technique, with less stringent requirements, is that of smoothing. Smoothing is used to create a function that approximates the most important points or features of the data~\citep{Savi64}. Thus, unlike interpolation, smoothing does not attempt to create a function that passes through every existing data point, but rather provides an approximation that highlights the most important features of the data.

A technique that can be used by both interpolation and smoothing is that of splining. Splines are used to define curves in a piecewise manner through the use of polynomials of a low degree~\citep{Mara10}. Through the use of this method, a curve would be decomposed into a number of splines which together describe the most important features of the data. Splines are used because they allow one to reduce the complexity of data by approximating complex curves in a way that is simple to construct and evaluate~\citep{Dier93}.

\subsubsection{Regression}
Another method that uses elements of both interpolation and smoothing is that of regression. Regression is a statistical technique used to ascertain the relationship between two or more variables~\citep{Syke00}. This is done by constructing a trend line or line of best fit. In general, this line is described by some function with the regression serving to determine the coefficients of this equation~\citep{Syke00}. As regression can be applied in a number of different ways an overview of the differences in accuracy and computational complexity will be discussed.

The main premise behind regression is that of constructing some simplified representation of the data that encapsulates the relationship between the variables~\citep{Syke00}. One of the simplest techniques to determine this relationship, is that of least squares. Least squares approximates the relationship between the variables by minimising the sum of squared distances between each data point and the trend line, thus providing a line of best fit~\citep{Tred02}. The method of minimising the sum of squared distance can be replaced with the minimisation of some other norm, however, this is the most commonly used technique in simple regression~\citep{Laws95}. This minimisation can either be done for only the dependant variables (ordinary least squares), or for all variables (total or geometric least squares). Thus, ordinary least squares almost always minimises the vertical offsets while total least squares minimises the perpendicular offsets in relation to the line of best fit~\citep{Laws95}. While total least squares offers a better line of fit for general data (as opposed to data investigating the relationship between independant and dependant variables), there is a significant performance cost associated with computing the gemoetric fit due to the addition of a second minimisation parameter. In addition, the complexity of the problem as well as computational cost increases considerable when attempting to fit multivariate equations~\citep{Laws95}. These constraints are true of multivariate regression in general~\citep{Syke00}.

A similar problem is faced when considering non-linear regression. Unlike its linear counterpart, non-linear regression attempts to fit data using one or more independant variables and a nonlinear combination of parameters~\citep{Syke00}. Computing non-linear least squares is done by using a linear approximation and refining the parameters through successive iterations. The need for multiple iterations over the data greatly increases the performance cost of such a technique~\citep{Laws95}.

The techniques discussed above have refered to parametric regression. That is, regression where the unknown paramaters of a specific function are calculated from the data. This requires some previous knowledge of what form the data takes, as the use of an incorrect fitting function can produce highly inaccurate or spurious results. Non-paramametric regression does not suffer from this problem to the same degree, as it determines both the model or function and the coefficients from the data~\citep{Syke00}. While the ability to fit data to a family of functions makes non-paramteric regression extremely powerful, it also greatly increases the number of data points required. This is due to the fact that both the paramaters and the model itself need to be calculated from the data. In addition, the computational cost of deriving the model, in addition to the parameters of this model, must be considered~\citep{Cord09}.

Thus while regression techniques can be extremely powerful in reducing the dimensionality of data, the computational and data requirements of each variation need to be carefully considered. That is, where possible, simpler techniques should be used to avoid the computational overhead associated with increased statistical complexity.


